ts on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Hawaii remains one of the most reliably Democratic states in presidential politics. It has sided with the Democratic nominee in every election since 1988, and the last two contests produced margins of roughly 22 and 29 points even as the nation as a whole stayed closely divided. With the Republican incumbent term-limited, the 2028 race is open on both sides, and no candidate carries an incumbency advantage into the general election.\n\nNo general-election polls or expert ratings for Hawaii have been published yet, so the forecast leans on the state's durable partisan record rather than campaign-level signals. That record points to a comfortable Democratic win in the mid-to-high teens, with wide uncertainty until actual polling arrives. Republican candidates rarely contest the state, and a dramatic national shift would be needed to put its four electoral votes in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}]}